==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|īleus}}, from {{der|en|grc|εἰλεός||intestinal obstruction}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪliəs/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪliəs|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es|ilei}}

# {{lb|en|medicine|modern usage}} [[disruption|Disruption]] of the [[normal]] [[propulsive]] [[ability]] of the [[gastrointestinal tract]], due to [[failure]] of [[peristalsis]].
# {{lb|en|medicine|broad definition|now rare}} [[disruption|Disruption]] of the [[normal]] [[propulsive]] [[ability]] of the [[gastrointestinal tract]] from any [[cause]].
#* '''1796''', {{w|Erasmus Darwin}}, ''[[s:Zoonomia/II.IV.III.II|Zoonomia]]'', Volume II: Classes of Diseases, IV: Diseases of Association, Ordo III: Retrograde Associate Motions, Genus II: Catenated with sensitive motions,
#*: The perpetual vomiting in '''ileus''' is caused in like manner by the defective excitement of the sensorial power of association by the bowel, which is torpid during the intervals of pain; and the stomach sympathizes with it.
#* '''1849''', {{w|Hippocrates}}, {{w|Francis Adams}} (translator), ''[[s:Aphorisms|Aphorisms]]'',
#*: When '''ileus''' comes on in a case of strangury, they prove fatal in seven days, unless, fever supervening, there be a copious discharge of urine.
#* '''1921''', Western Osteopathic Association, et al., ''The Western osteopath'', Volume 16, [http://books.google.com/books?id=BdwHAQAAIAAJ&q=%22ileuses%22&dq=%22ileuses%22&hl=en&ei=16c-Tr33LsXOmAX2jdWOCA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&sqi=2&ved=0CDIQ6AEwAg page 20],
#*: You must expect to lose from 40% to 60% of all acute post operative '''ileuses''', and about 20% of your sub-acute cases.
#* '''2002''', Douglas H. Slatter, ''Textbook of Small Animal Surgery'', Volume 1, [http://books.google.com/books?id=B_nh6zSr4wUC&pg=PA420&dq=%22ileus|ileuses%22&hl=en&ei=BqI-TpO5HKX0mAXFu_GTCA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CCcQ6AEwADgo#v=onepage&q=%22ileus|ileuses%22&f=false page 420],
#*: Thus, '''ileus''' also has potential detrimental effects in sterile peritonitis by providing a source and mechanism for entry of bacteria into the peritoneal cavity.
#*: Other factors commonly encountered in animals with peritonitis may also predispose to adynamic '''ileus'''.

